{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787060652,"nonce":"MYtvyHhPjfsmFmaZOxLfk5WbDHc=","title":"Storm and flapping damping make oto decide not to speak, and a suppressed notification is indistinguishable from a signal that never fired","message":"**What is wrong.** `notifications.suppressed_reason` admits `no_policy | throttled | storm | flapping`. The\nlast two are oto choosing to withhold a notification about a real signal, on its own judgement. That makes\nthe product's output ambiguous in the worst possible direction: an operator who was not told cannot tell\nwhether nothing happened or whether oto decided the event was not worth mentioning.\n\n**The demonstration.** `internal/notification/domain/broadcast.go:148` (`WarrantsChannelNotice`) and\n`internal/notification/domain/mode.go:298,304,367,369` branch on `StormMode` and `Flapping` to change or\nwithhold what is sent. `alerts.is_flapping`'s own column comment names the standard the current behaviour\nfails: \"A VISIBLE UI state, never silent suppression -- silence destroys trust (SPEC §B.6)\".\n\n**Why it matters.** oto's promise is to report faithfully what a signal did. Damping at delivery breaks that\npromise silently, and silence is the one failure an alerting product cannot be forgiven for. Both cases have\nbetter homes: flapping noise is removed at CASE FORMATION by a retention window, so the notifications never\nexist to suppress; and a storm is a real event that deserves to be seen, not folded. Flooding a channel is a\ntruthful signal that something is badly wrong. Once incidents exist (`correlation`, DEFERRED-POST-V1), a\nstorm is what CREATES an incident and the notification belongs on the incident — that is storm's layer, and\nit is not this one.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql` (`suppressed_reason`) ·\n`internal/notification/domain/broadcast.go:148` · `internal/notification/domain/mode.go:298,304,367,369` ·\n`internal/grouping/domain/group.go` (`storm_mode`, `storm_since`, `EvaluateStorm`) ·\n`internal/channels/domain/ports.go:146` (`ModeBroadcastReply`) · `docs/design/SPEC.md` §B.6, §H.6.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.**\n1. `suppressed_reason` admits `no_policy` and `throttled` only. Neither is oto's opinion: the first means\n   there is nowhere to send, the second is a provider's rate limit, which is the world's constraint.\n2. No notification is withheld or downgraded because a group is in storm mode or an alert is flapping.\n   A test asserts a storm produces one notification per triggering change, not a collapsed digest.\n3. `storm_mode` and `storm_since` REMAIN, and remain visible. Storm detection is a true fact about a group\n   and is not what is being removed.\n4. `ModEBroadcastReply` and `WarrantsChannelNotice` REMAIN. A channel-level notice makes a storm MORE\n   visible, which is the opposite of damping and is consistent with this ticket rather than in tension.\n5. The flapping digest is deleted. It depends on the case-retention ticket landing first, which removes the\n   noise it existed to manage; landing this one alone would flood.\n6. SPEC §B.6 records that a storm's notification home is the incident, once incidents exist, and that oto\n   does not dampen in the meantime.","files":null}]}